                <pb facs="0513" n="439" sameAs="frus:frus1941-43/pg439"/>

                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Editorial Note</hi>
                </head>
                <p>No official record of the substance of this conversation has been found. The
                    information set forth above is derived from the President’s appointment calendar
                    (Roosevelt Papers). Eisenhower’s own brief account (Dwight D. Eisenhower, <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Crusade in Europe</hi> (Garden City, New York: Doubleday
                    &amp; Company, Inc., 1948), p. 51) refers to this meeting as “no more than an
                    informal chat” which had “no military significance”. Churchill, in his account
                    in <hi rendition="#italic">Hinge of Fate</hi>, pp. 384–385, recalls meeting
                    alone with Eisenhower and Clark late on the afternoon of June 21, 1942.
                    Churchill further recalls that in the course of the meeting, which lasted over
                    an hour, he gave the American generals a copy of his memorandum of June 15,
                    1942, on <hi rendition="#small-caps">Round-Up</hi>. Churchill’s memorandum is
                    printed <hi rendition="#italic">post</hi>, <ref target="#pg455">p.
                    455</ref>.</p>
            </div>
